{"line":412,"clause":"L412","pdf_page":"9","status":"FALSE","excerpt":"Replacing $q$ by $-q$ we may assume $B$ is positive definite, and completing the square $q(w)=(w-w_0)^TB(w-w_0)+v$. Let $\\mu_1,\\mu_2>0$ be the eigenvalues of $B$ with orthonormal eigenvectors $e_1,e_2$. The map $(\\delta,\\theta)\\mapsto w_0+\\sqrt{\\delta/\\mu_1}\\cos\\theta\\,e_1+\\sqrt{\\delta/\\mu_2}\\sin\\theta\\,e_2$ is a diffeomorphism of $(0,\\infty)\\times[0,2\\pi)$ onto $\\R^2\\setminus\\{w_0\\}$ with $q=v+\\delta$ and Jacobian $1/(2\\sqrt{\\det B})$. Hence $q(W)$ has density $(2\\sqrt{\\det B})^{-1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}p_W(w(\\delta,\\theta))\\dd\\theta\\le\\pi L/\\sqrt{\\det B}$ at $v+\\delta$, and density zero below $v$. This is a change of variables in a deterministic integral; no independence after rotation is used.","hypotheses":"As printed","derivation":"The claimed global diffeomorphism from (0,infinity)x[0,2pi) to the punctured plane is not literally true because of the angular seam. On (0,infinity)x(0,2pi) it is a diffeomorphism onto a slit plane; omitted sets are null, the Jacobian is 1/(2sqrt(det B)), and the density bound survives.","dependencies":"C01","downstream":"Local wording defect; repaired Lemma 5.2 passes.","source_commit":"ec211ba2d77a4a9850168c6af1574ea651feaa7c","source_tex_blob":"c4f2a997cf336ee974e15409b5bccdd8cca039d8","source_pdf_sha256":"4c1a8eb808b32739625aa475c1aad24dc857a3f35fc960504b371487f2850423","account":"account03","slot":"B12","run_id":"20260925T014656Z-account03-B12-lineaudit-r68","pass_id":"P1-B12-forward-from-line1","pdf_render_note":"Page mapping follows package anchors; direct screenshot unavailable."}
